<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Idrimi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Idrimi was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amuq-valley">Amuq plain</a> in the 15th century BCE, a refugee prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> who took power with the help of Habiru warriors and ruled as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>. His life story is inscribed on a seated statue found at Alalakh in 1939 and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. It is one of the few autobiographical texts of the period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/idrimi.jpg" alt="Idrimi" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_King_Idrimi-IMG_4553-black.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to his inscription, Idrimi was a younger son of Ilim-Ilimma I of Halab, and his mother's family were connected with the lords of Emar on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. After what the text calls a crime in his father's house, the family fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> to Emar, where Idrimi said that whoever lived among its people was a slave. Leaving his brothers, he took his horse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> and squire into the desert and joined the Habiru at Ammiya in the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, where refugees from Halab, Mukish, Nihi and Nuhašše gathered around him.</p><p>In the seventh year, the inscription says, the storm-god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teshub">Teshub</a> turned towards him. He built ships and brought troops by sea to Mukish, and Ni'i, Mukish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a> came over to him in a single day. His brothers then joined him, and they swore a mutual alliance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Idrimi sent the envoy Anwanda to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barattarna">Barattarna</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, who had been hostile to him for seven years, recalling the old treaties between the kings of Halab and the Hurrian kings. Barattarna accepted his tribute, and Idrimi swore an oath as a loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The historian Paul Collins notes that Mitanni left him limited freedom of action as long as he did not cross its policy.</p><p>The inscription says he attacked Hittite territory, destroyed seven cities and built a palace from the booty. A treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pilliya">Pilliya</a> of Kizzuwatna, another <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barattarna">Barattarna</a>, provided for the exchange of fugitives and set rewards of five hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> shekels for a returned man and one thousand for a woman. His seal was later used by his son and successor Niqmepa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley</a> found the statue in a pit of a later temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Tell Atchana</a>. Its 104 lines are written in a provincial dialect of Akkadian, and Jacob Lauinger dates the inscription to around 1400 to 1350 BCE, after Idrimi's lifetime. Many scholars read it as pseudo-history or a moralising tale, and Tremper Longman finds in it the threefold structure of Akkadian fictional autobiography. Adolf Oppenheim compared its pattern of sevens with the story of King David, and Edward Greenstein with those of Jacob, Joseph and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Idrimi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Further light comes from the inscriptions of <b>Idrimi</b>, the 15th-century BCE king of Alalakh, and the tablets of Emar of the 14th and 13th centuries BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>After a Hittite destruction c. 1600 BCE, <b>Idrimi</b>, a refugee prince from Aleppo, took the city and ruled Mukish as a vassal of the Mitanni Empire, as his inscribed statue relates.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mukish">Kingdom of Mukish</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings, such as Isputahsu, Pilliya and Sunassura, concluded treaties with Hittite rulers and with <b>Idrimi</b> of Mukish.</blockquote><a href="/state/kizzuwatna">Kizzuwatna</a></li></ul></section>
